llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
116): GATED_DELTA_NET with head size 128 runs prompt batches on a kernel
that gives each state column to two lanes instead of a whole wave.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
In a 2048-token prompt batch of Qwen3.6 35B-A3B, rocprof puts each
GATED_DELTA_NET call at about 44 ms; the 30 calls (one per DeltaNet layer)
take 1.71 s of about 6.6 s of GPU kernel time, 26%. The arithmetic is
small: per token and head, three multiply-add passes over the 128 x 128
state (kv = S k, the update S = g S + k delta, the output S q) and one
scaling pass, about 225 GFLOP for the batch, which the RX 590's 7.1 fp32
TFLOPS cover in about 32 ms. The existing kernel gives each state column
one wave and walks the tokens one after another: per token every lane
loads k, q, v, the gate and beta from global memory, and the wave does two
wave-wide reductions around a few multiply-adds per lane, so the shuffles
and the serial load latency set the pace.

Measured on an RX 590 (llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, all experts in
system RAM with -ncmoe 99, pp2048 with 2048-token batches, 3 repetitions,
two interleaved rounds, page cache dropped before each run,
HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1 ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1): 341.50 +- 34.93 and
361.16 +- 1.86 tok/s without the patch, 454.99 +- 3.26 and 455.65 +- 1.37
tok/s with it (+26%). Generation is unchanged, because single-token decode
keeps the existing kernel.

WHAT
----
launch_gated_delta_net runs gated_delta_net_col_cuda when n_tokens > 1 and
S_v == 128. Two neighbouring lanes own one state column, 64 rows each held
in registers: lane h of the pair holds rows 8m + 4h + e (m < 16, e < 4), so
the column is read and written as float4 and both lanes read neighbouring
float4s of the same shared-memory row. A dot product over the column is a
sequential sum over the lane's 64 rows plus one exchange with the
neighbour: a DPP move (quad_perm [1,0,3,2]) on HIP, __shfl_xor_sync on
CUDA; x + y == y + x, so both lanes hold the same bits afterwards. A block
of 128 threads takes 64 columns; the grid is (heads, sequences, 2). Each
block stages a chunk of tokens in shared memory with coalesced loads: k_t
and q_t (128 floats each), v_t for its 64 columns, beta_t and expf(g_t)
(the whole gate row with KDA, so expf runs once per element rather than
once per lane). A chunk is 16 tokens (20.6 KB) or 8 with KDA (14.4 KB),
under 32 KB so that two blocks share a CU. Per token a lane reads k and q
as broadcast loads and updates its 64 rows in registers. On gfx803 the
token loop compiles to v_mac_f32 and v_mul_f32 with no v_fma_f32 (a
quarter-rate instruction there), at 159-165 VGPRs and no scratch;
__launch_bounds__(128, 1) allows that register count, which means one wave
per SIMD, and the four waves of two blocks occupy a CU's four SIMDs.

The kernel keeps every variant of the existing one: KDA, the snapshot
slots of keep_rs_t, several sequences, the GQA head mapping, the fused
snapshot store into the cache (ggml_cuda_try_gdn_cache_fusion), and 114's
state_ids_t variant that reads the state from cache row ids[seq]. Reading
and storing the same cache row is safe here as well: each lane reads all
of its rows before it writes any, and no lane writes another lane's rows.
Single tokens, head sizes 16, 32 and 64, and a state input, state output,
snapshot-slot stride or cache-row stride that is not 16-byte aligned run
the existing kernel.

Numerics: the per-element expressions are those of the existing kernel;
only the summation order of the 128-long dot products changes (64 rows in
sequence and one pair add instead of a tree across the wave), so results
are not bit-identical. test-backend-ops GATED_DELTA_NET passes 47 of 47
cases against the CPU backend at NMSE 1e-7 with either kernel, including
head-size-128 cases added for the check (1 to 512 tokens, across the chunk
boundaries, two sequences, GQA, permuted inputs, KDA, 4 snapshot slots,
the cache-store fusion). Against CPU-computed logits (llama-perplexity, 8
chunks of 512 tokens in 512-token batches, q8_0 KV cache) the two kernels
are equivalent: mean KLD 0.0104 +- 0.0005 with the existing kernel and
0.0107 +- 0.0005 with this one, PPL 13.381 and 13.384 (CPU 13.355), same
top token 95.8% and 95.3% (+- 0.45). Between the two kernels directly the
mean KLD is 0.0037. The recurrence carries every rounding difference
forward through the tokens; both GPU kernels sit about 0.01 KLD from the
CPU, and this one stays within that spread.

